1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,059
If you turned thirty in nineteen seventy-nine,

2
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:05,520
you were born in nineteen forty-nine,

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,515
right at the start of the baby

4
00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:07,848
boom.

5
00:00:08,028 --> 00:00:10,227
You grew up with Howdy Doody and

6
00:00:10,227 --> 00:00:11,464
the polio vaccine.

7
00:00:11,644 --> 00:00:13,896
You were a teenager when the Beatles

8
00:00:13,896 --> 00:00:14,396
arrived.

9
00:00:14,576 --> 00:00:16,821
You came of age in the sixties,

10
00:00:16,821 --> 00:00:20,079
with Vietnam, the moon landing and Woodstock.

11
00:00:20,259 --> 00:00:21,610
And now, at thirty,

12
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:24,668
you were facing something nobody had warned

13
00:00:24,668 --> 00:00:25,379
you about.

14
00:00:25,559 --> 00:00:27,974
Being a grown-up in the late seventies.

15
00:00:28,323 --> 00:00:30,727
There's an old saying from the sixties.

16
00:00:30,907 --> 00:00:33,031
Don't trust anyone over thirty.

17
00:00:33,211 --> 00:00:34,928
In nineteen seventy-nine,

18
00:00:34,928 --> 00:00:37,057
the people who had said it were

19
00:00:37,057 --> 00:00:38,842
turning thirty themselves.

20
00:00:39,022 --> 00:00:41,347
The first wave of baby boomers was

21
00:00:41,347 --> 00:00:43,604
settling down, getting mortgages,

22
00:00:43,604 --> 00:00:46,271
raising kids and wondering how they had

23
00:00:46,271 --> 00:00:47,706
gotten there so fast.

24
00:00:48,057 --> 00:00:50,170
So what was life really like at

25
00:00:50,170 --> 00:00:52,352
thirty in nineteen seventy-nine?

26
00:00:52,532 --> 00:00:54,134
The job, the house,

27
00:00:54,134 --> 00:00:55,988
the family, the money,

28
00:00:55,988 --> 00:00:57,083
and the news.

29
00:00:57,263 --> 00:00:59,171
It was a harder time than many

30
00:00:59,171 --> 00:01:00,189
people remember.

31
00:01:00,369 --> 00:01:02,152
But it was also a time of

32
00:01:02,152 --> 00:01:03,864
real freedom and change.

33
00:01:04,044 --> 00:01:05,170
Let's go back.

34
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:06,704
There were a lot of them.

35
00:01:06,884 --> 00:01:08,639
In nineteen seventy-nine,

36
00:01:08,639 --> 00:01:11,027
more than three and a half million

37
00:01:11,027 --> 00:01:12,712
Americans turned thirty,

38
00:01:12,712 --> 00:01:15,100
part of a generation so large that

39
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:17,839
every stage of their lives reshaped the

40
00:01:17,839 --> 00:01:18,401
country.

41
00:01:18,581 --> 00:01:20,008
When they were babies,

42
00:01:20,008 --> 00:01:22,215
the country built maternity wards.

43
00:01:22,395 --> 00:01:24,094
When they were children,

44
00:01:24,094 --> 00:01:25,297
it built schools.

45
00:01:25,477 --> 00:01:27,559
And now that they were thirty,

46
00:01:27,559 --> 00:01:30,058
everyone wanted to sell them houses,

47
00:01:30,058 --> 00:01:31,723
cars and life insurance.

48
00:01:32,074 --> 00:01:35,353
By thirty, most people in nineteen seventy-nine

49
00:01:35,353 --> 00:01:37,796
had been working for about eight to

50
00:01:37,796 --> 00:01:38,494
ten years.

51
00:01:38,674 --> 00:01:40,884
Many men had served in Vietnam,

52
00:01:40,884 --> 00:01:43,165
or had a draft number that never

53
00:01:43,165 --> 00:01:43,736
came up.

54
00:01:43,916 --> 00:01:47,243
Many had used college deferments and graduated

55
00:01:47,243 --> 00:01:48,907
in the early seventies,

56
00:01:48,907 --> 00:01:51,439
when good jobs were easier to find.

57
00:01:51,619 --> 00:01:54,305
Others had gone straight from high school

58
00:01:54,305 --> 00:01:55,288
to the factory.

59
00:01:55,638 --> 00:01:57,802
But the economy of the late seventies

60
00:01:57,802 --> 00:01:58,621
was difficult.

61
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:01,727
Factories in the Northeast and the Midwest

62
00:02:01,727 --> 00:02:03,329
were starting to close,

63
00:02:03,329 --> 00:02:05,907
as companies moved south or overseas.

64
00:02:06,087 --> 00:02:09,338
Steel mills in Ohio and Pennsylvania were

65
00:02:09,338 --> 00:02:10,765
laying people off.

66
00:02:10,945 --> 00:02:12,754
In nineteen seventy-nine,

67
00:02:12,754 --> 00:02:15,721
Chrysler was nearly bankrupt and needed a

68
00:02:15,721 --> 00:02:19,049
loan guarantee from the government to survive.

69
00:02:19,399 --> 00:02:20,614
For women, though,

70
00:02:20,614 --> 00:02:22,839
the late seventies were a time of

71
00:02:22,839 --> 00:02:23,649
opportunity.

72
00:02:23,829 --> 00:02:25,504
By nineteen seventy-nine,

73
00:02:25,504 --> 00:02:28,051
about half of all married women worked

74
00:02:28,051 --> 00:02:29,190
outside the home,

75
00:02:29,190 --> 00:02:32,072
far more than in their mothers' generation.

76
00:02:32,252 --> 00:02:34,465
Women were becoming doctors,

77
00:02:34,465 --> 00:02:38,260
lawyers, managers and police officers in growing

78
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:38,893
numbers.

79
00:02:39,073 --> 00:02:41,759
Many thirty-year-old women were the first in

80
00:02:41,759 --> 00:02:44,019
their families to have a real career.

81
00:02:44,368 --> 00:02:47,772
The office of nineteen seventy-nine was full

82
00:02:47,772 --> 00:02:50,789
of smoke, typewriters and carbon paper.

83
00:02:50,969 --> 00:02:53,349
The IBM Selectric typewriter,

84
00:02:53,349 --> 00:02:55,647
with its spinning golf ball,

85
00:02:55,647 --> 00:02:58,028
was the height of technology.

86
00:02:58,208 --> 00:03:01,087
Copies were made on a Xerox machine.

87
00:03:01,267 --> 00:03:04,267
Calculators had just gotten cheap enough for

88
00:03:04,267 --> 00:03:05,017
every desk.

89
00:03:05,197 --> 00:03:07,070
And if you needed to send a

90
00:03:07,070 --> 00:03:09,984
document quickly, you used a telex machine

91
00:03:09,984 --> 00:03:10,816
or the mail.

92
00:03:11,166 --> 00:03:14,424
The biggest problem of nineteen seventy-nine was

93
00:03:14,424 --> 00:03:15,102
inflation.

94
00:03:15,282 --> 00:03:17,705
Prices were rising by more than eleven

95
00:03:17,705 --> 00:03:18,661
percent a year.

96
00:03:18,841 --> 00:03:20,037
By the next year,

97
00:03:20,037 --> 00:03:22,428
it would be over thirteen percent.

98
00:03:22,608 --> 00:03:25,521
A paycheck that seemed fine in January

99
00:03:25,521 --> 00:03:27,438
felt smaller by December.

100
00:03:27,618 --> 00:03:30,823
Families watched the price of groceries,

101
00:03:30,823 --> 00:03:33,307
gas and rent climb every month.

102
00:03:33,657 --> 00:03:35,591
In nineteen seventy-nine,

103
00:03:35,591 --> 00:03:38,067
a loaf of bread cost about fifty

104
00:03:38,067 --> 00:03:40,465
cents, a gallon of milk about a

105
00:03:40,465 --> 00:03:42,322
dollar and seventy-five,

106
00:03:42,322 --> 00:03:44,798
and a new car about six thousand

107
00:03:44,798 --> 00:03:45,417
dollars.

108
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:48,302
The median family income was around nineteen

109
00:03:48,302 --> 00:03:49,777
thousand dollars a year.

110
00:03:49,957 --> 00:03:52,418
People felt richer than their parents,

111
00:03:52,418 --> 00:03:54,102
but somehow always behind.

112
00:03:54,452 --> 00:03:56,750
And savings didn't help much.

113
00:03:56,930 --> 00:04:00,057
Money in a passbook savings account earned

114
00:04:00,057 --> 00:04:01,546
around five percent,

115
00:04:01,546 --> 00:04:04,152
while prices rose more than eleven.

116
00:04:04,332 --> 00:04:07,128
People learned that holding cash meant losing

117
00:04:07,128 --> 00:04:07,501
money.

118
00:04:07,681 --> 00:04:10,061
So many bought things instead.

119
00:04:10,241 --> 00:04:12,128
A house, a car,

120
00:04:12,128 --> 00:04:14,769
a stereo, a color TV.

121
00:04:14,949 --> 00:04:18,233
Credit cards, like Visa and MasterCard,

122
00:04:18,233 --> 00:04:21,265
became common in the late seventies.

123
00:04:21,615 --> 00:04:23,995
Taxes were another frustration.

124
00:04:24,175 --> 00:04:26,680
As inflation pushed paychecks higher,

125
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:29,253
people moved into higher tax brackets,

126
00:04:29,253 --> 00:04:31,826
even though their money bought no more

127
00:04:31,826 --> 00:04:32,638
than before.

128
00:04:32,818 --> 00:04:34,978
People called it bracket creep.

129
00:04:35,158 --> 00:04:37,045
In nineteen seventy-eight,

130
00:04:37,045 --> 00:04:40,384
California voters passed Proposition Thirteen,

131
00:04:40,384 --> 00:04:42,489
which slashed property taxes,

132
00:04:42,489 --> 00:04:44,956
and a tax revolt spread across the

133
00:04:44,956 --> 00:04:45,537
country.

134
00:04:45,887 --> 00:04:47,876
Buying a first house at thirty in

135
00:04:47,876 --> 00:04:50,528
nineteen seventy-nine was harder than it had

136
00:04:50,528 --> 00:04:51,854
been for your parents.

137
00:04:52,034 --> 00:04:53,786
The median price of a new home

138
00:04:53,786 --> 00:04:55,947
was about sixty-two thousand dollars.

139
00:04:56,127 --> 00:04:58,683
And mortgage rates were climbing fast,

140
00:04:58,683 --> 00:05:01,105
passing eleven percent by the end of

141
00:05:01,105 --> 00:05:01,711
the year.

142
00:05:01,891 --> 00:05:03,033
Within two years,

143
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:05,316
they would reach eighteen percent.

144
00:05:05,666 --> 00:05:08,678
Many young couples needed both incomes to

145
00:05:08,678 --> 00:05:10,368
qualify for a mortgage.

146
00:05:10,548 --> 00:05:12,810
Some bought old houses in the city

147
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:14,740
and fixed them up themselves,

148
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:16,736
a trend that came to be called

149
00:05:16,736 --> 00:05:17,734
gentrification.

150
00:05:17,914 --> 00:05:20,484
Others bought condos or townhouses,

151
00:05:20,484 --> 00:05:22,686
which were new in many places.

152
00:05:22,866 --> 00:05:24,886
And many simply kept renting,

153
00:05:24,886 --> 00:05:25,652
and waited.

154
00:05:26,003 --> 00:05:27,191
Inside the house,

155
00:05:27,191 --> 00:05:29,567
the style was pure late seventies.

156
00:05:29,747 --> 00:05:31,523
Earth tones everywhere.

157
00:05:31,703 --> 00:05:34,873
Brown, orange, gold and green.

158
00:05:35,053 --> 00:05:36,190
Shag carpet.

159
00:05:36,370 --> 00:05:37,497
Wood paneling.

160
00:05:37,677 --> 00:05:39,488
Macrame plant hangers.

161
00:05:39,668 --> 00:05:42,396
A waterbed, if you were adventurous.

162
00:05:42,576 --> 00:05:45,440
A stereo system with big speakers and

163
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,917
an eight-track or cassette deck.

164
00:05:48,097 --> 00:05:49,775
And a new microwave oven,

165
00:05:49,775 --> 00:05:52,729
which many families bought around that time.

166
00:05:53,079 --> 00:05:55,261
And the kitchen had changed too.

167
00:05:55,441 --> 00:05:58,820
Avocado and harvest gold appliances.

168
00:05:59,000 --> 00:05:59,696
A Mr.

169
00:05:59,876 --> 00:06:02,065
Coffee machine on the counter,

170
00:06:02,065 --> 00:06:05,275
which Joe DiMaggio advertised on television.

171
00:06:05,455 --> 00:06:08,009
A Crock-Pot, the slow cooker that let

172
00:06:08,009 --> 00:06:10,976
working parents start dinner in the morning

173
00:06:10,976 --> 00:06:12,909
and come home to a hot meal.

174
00:06:13,089 --> 00:06:14,371
And a fondue pot,

175
00:06:14,371 --> 00:06:15,881
from a wedding gift,

176
00:06:15,881 --> 00:06:17,164
used maybe twice.

177
00:06:17,514 --> 00:06:20,685
By thirty, many people in nineteen seventy-nine

178
00:06:20,685 --> 00:06:22,912
were married with young children.

179
00:06:23,092 --> 00:06:24,706
But families were changing.

180
00:06:24,886 --> 00:06:27,088
Couples were marrying a little later and

181
00:06:27,088 --> 00:06:29,344
having fewer children than their parents.

182
00:06:29,524 --> 00:06:32,348
The average family had about two kids,

183
00:06:32,348 --> 00:06:34,874
compared with three or four in the

184
00:06:34,874 --> 00:06:35,469
fifties.

185
00:06:35,819 --> 00:06:38,288
Divorce was also much more common than

186
00:06:38,288 --> 00:06:40,172
in their parents' generation.

187
00:06:40,352 --> 00:06:43,122
In the late seventies and early eighties,

188
00:06:43,122 --> 00:06:45,756
the divorce rate in America reached its

189
00:06:45,756 --> 00:06:47,040
highest point ever.

190
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:50,530
Many thirty-year-olds had already been married and

191
00:06:50,530 --> 00:06:51,457
divorced once.

192
00:06:51,637 --> 00:06:55,830
Single parents, stepfamilies and joint custody became

193
00:06:55,830 --> 00:06:57,570
part of everyday life.

194
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:01,577
Raising kids in nineteen seventy-nine meant Sesame

195
00:07:01,577 --> 00:07:03,917
Street, which was ten years old,

196
00:07:03,917 --> 00:07:05,234
and Mister Rogers.

197
00:07:05,414 --> 00:07:07,041
It meant Star Wars toys,

198
00:07:07,041 --> 00:07:09,752
which every child wanted after the movie

199
00:07:09,752 --> 00:07:12,124
came out in nineteen seventy-seven.

200
00:07:12,304 --> 00:07:14,501
And it meant less worry than parents

201
00:07:14,501 --> 00:07:15,172
feel today.

202
00:07:15,352 --> 00:07:17,302
Kids still played outside,

203
00:07:17,302 --> 00:07:18,727
rode bikes all day,

204
00:07:18,727 --> 00:07:21,053
and came home when it got dark.

205
00:07:21,403 --> 00:07:23,806
And childcare was a new worry.

206
00:07:23,986 --> 00:07:25,865
With both parents working,

207
00:07:25,865 --> 00:07:28,177
many young families needed help.

208
00:07:28,357 --> 00:07:30,834
Day care centers were opening across the

209
00:07:30,834 --> 00:07:31,329
country.

210
00:07:31,509 --> 00:07:34,152
Some families relied on grandparents,

211
00:07:34,152 --> 00:07:37,152
neighbors or a babysitter down the street.

212
00:07:37,332 --> 00:07:40,188
Latchkey kids came home to empty houses

213
00:07:40,188 --> 00:07:41,140
after school.

214
00:07:41,320 --> 00:07:43,079
It was a big change from the

215
00:07:43,079 --> 00:07:46,408
way the thirty-year-olds of nineteen seventy-nine had

216
00:07:46,408 --> 00:07:46,974
grown up.

217
00:07:47,324 --> 00:07:49,924
Then, in the spring and summer of

218
00:07:49,924 --> 00:07:53,628
nineteen seventy-nine, the gas lines came back.

219
00:07:53,808 --> 00:07:57,233
A revolution in Iran cut oil supplies,

220
00:07:57,233 --> 00:07:58,765
and panic set in.

221
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:02,382
Lines at gas stations stretched for blocks.

222
00:08:02,562 --> 00:08:04,849
Some stations ran out completely.

223
00:08:05,029 --> 00:08:06,205
In many states,

224
00:08:06,205 --> 00:08:08,480
you could only buy gas on odd

225
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:09,499
or even days,

226
00:08:09,499 --> 00:08:12,244
depending on the last digit of your

227
00:08:12,244 --> 00:08:13,342
license plate.

228
00:08:13,692 --> 00:08:15,682
People waited for hours,

229
00:08:15,682 --> 00:08:17,507
sometimes before dawn,

230
00:08:17,507 --> 00:08:18,835
just to fill up.

231
00:08:19,015 --> 00:08:20,118
Tempers flared.

232
00:08:20,298 --> 00:08:22,777
Gas prices passed a dollar a gallon

233
00:08:22,777 --> 00:08:25,185
in many places for the first time.

234
00:08:25,365 --> 00:08:28,336
Americans started buying smaller cars,

235
00:08:28,336 --> 00:08:30,056
especially from Japan.

236
00:08:30,236 --> 00:08:34,381
Hondas, Toyotas and Datsuns were suddenly everywhere.

237
00:08:34,561 --> 00:08:36,662
Detroit was caught off guard.

238
00:08:37,012 --> 00:08:39,884
The family car of a thirty-year-old in

239
00:08:39,884 --> 00:08:43,210
nineteen seventy-nine might be a Ford Pinto,

240
00:08:43,210 --> 00:08:44,495
a Chevy Chevette,

241
00:08:44,495 --> 00:08:45,629
an AMC Gremlin,

242
00:08:45,629 --> 00:08:47,368
or a Volkswagen Rabbit.

243
00:08:47,548 --> 00:08:48,649
If you had kids,

244
00:08:48,649 --> 00:08:51,472
maybe a station wagon with wood paneling.

245
00:08:51,652 --> 00:08:53,807
Many of those cars were not well

246
00:08:53,807 --> 00:08:56,365
made, and rust was a constant problem.

247
00:08:56,545 --> 00:08:58,889
But gas mileage was now the number

248
00:08:58,889 --> 00:09:00,957
one question at every car lot.

249
00:09:01,308 --> 00:09:03,698
In July of nineteen seventy-nine,

250
00:09:03,698 --> 00:09:06,814
President Jimmy Carter gave a famous speech

251
00:09:06,814 --> 00:09:09,422
on television about what he called a

252
00:09:09,422 --> 00:09:10,944
crisis of confidence.

253
00:09:11,124 --> 00:09:13,564
He said Americans were losing faith in

254
00:09:13,564 --> 00:09:14,270
the future.

255
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:16,691
The press called it the malaise speech.

256
00:09:16,871 --> 00:09:19,742
Many thirty-year-olds felt exactly what he was

257
00:09:19,742 --> 00:09:22,489
describing, even if they didn't like hearing

258
00:09:22,489 --> 00:09:22,676
it.

259
00:09:23,026 --> 00:09:27,089
Nineteen seventy-nine was full of unsettling news.

260
00:09:27,269 --> 00:09:29,949
In March, there was a partial meltdown

261
00:09:29,949 --> 00:09:32,630
at the Three Mile Island nuclear plant

262
00:09:32,630 --> 00:09:34,746
near Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.

263
00:09:34,926 --> 00:09:38,072
Thousands of families with young children left

264
00:09:38,072 --> 00:09:38,687
the area.

265
00:09:38,867 --> 00:09:39,967
That same month,

266
00:09:39,967 --> 00:09:42,647
a movie about a nuclear accident called

267
00:09:42,647 --> 00:09:45,601
The China Syndrome was playing in theaters.

268
00:09:45,781 --> 00:09:47,267
The timing was eerie.

269
00:09:47,618 --> 00:09:50,871
In July, the American space station Skylab

270
00:09:50,871 --> 00:09:53,195
fell out of orbit and broke up

271
00:09:53,195 --> 00:09:56,604
over the Indian Ocean and Western Australia.

272
00:09:56,784 --> 00:09:58,855
For weeks, people joked about where it

273
00:09:58,855 --> 00:09:59,454
would land.

274
00:09:59,634 --> 00:10:00,886
And in November,

275
00:10:00,886 --> 00:10:04,562
Iranian students took more than fifty Americans

276
00:10:04,562 --> 00:10:05,892
hostage at the U.

277
00:10:06,072 --> 00:10:06,420
S.

278
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:07,900
embassy in Tehran.

279
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:10,179
They would be held for four hundred

280
00:10:10,179 --> 00:10:11,378
and forty-four days.

281
00:10:11,728 --> 00:10:14,411
Every night, the news counted the days

282
00:10:14,411 --> 00:10:15,965
of the hostage crisis.

283
00:10:16,145 --> 00:10:19,268
Yellow ribbons appeared on trees across the

284
00:10:19,268 --> 00:10:19,849
country.

285
00:10:20,029 --> 00:10:22,664
People felt frustrated and helpless.

286
00:10:22,844 --> 00:10:24,387
It was a hard way to end

287
00:10:24,387 --> 00:10:26,701
a decade, and many people who turned

288
00:10:26,701 --> 00:10:29,079
thirty that year remember it clearly.

289
00:10:29,429 --> 00:10:32,400
But life at thirty in nineteen seventy-nine

290
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,782
wasn't all bad news.

291
00:10:33,962 --> 00:10:35,042
There was disco.

292
00:10:35,222 --> 00:10:37,982
Saturday Night Fever had come out in

293
00:10:37,982 --> 00:10:41,356
nineteen seventy-seven, and by seventy-nine,

294
00:10:41,356 --> 00:10:42,966
disco was everywhere.

295
00:10:43,146 --> 00:10:45,498
Couples went out on Saturday night to

296
00:10:45,498 --> 00:10:47,151
dance under a mirror ball.

297
00:10:47,331 --> 00:10:49,594
Donna Summer, the Bee Gees,

298
00:10:49,594 --> 00:10:52,610
Gloria Gaynor and Chic were on every

299
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:53,113
radio.

300
00:10:53,463 --> 00:10:56,388
But in July of nineteen seventy-nine,

301
00:10:56,388 --> 00:10:58,918
a radio DJ in Chicago held Disco

302
00:10:58,918 --> 00:11:01,606
Demolition Night at Comiskey Park,

303
00:11:01,606 --> 00:11:04,373
blowing up a crate of disco records

304
00:11:04,373 --> 00:11:07,615
between games of a baseball doubleheader.

305
00:11:07,795 --> 00:11:09,615
The crowd stormed the field,

306
00:11:09,615 --> 00:11:11,824
and the second game was forfeited.

307
00:11:12,004 --> 00:11:14,136
It was a sign that the disco

308
00:11:14,136 --> 00:11:15,278
era was ending.

309
00:11:15,458 --> 00:11:18,197
Rock, new wave and country were coming

310
00:11:18,197 --> 00:11:18,558
back.

311
00:11:18,908 --> 00:11:24,716
At home, thirty-year-olds watched Mork and Mindy,

312
00:11:24,716 --> 00:11:30,169
Taxi, M*A*S*H, Dallas and Saturday Night Live.

313
00:11:30,349 --> 00:11:33,625
In September, a new cable channel called

314
00:11:33,625 --> 00:11:35,345
ESPN went on the air,

315
00:11:35,345 --> 00:11:37,965
showing sports around the clock.

316
00:11:38,145 --> 00:11:41,234
Video cassette recorders were just appearing,

317
00:11:41,234 --> 00:11:42,813
expensive, but amazing.

318
00:11:42,993 --> 00:11:44,284
For the first time,

319
00:11:44,284 --> 00:11:46,526
you could record a show and watch

320
00:11:46,526 --> 00:11:47,137
it later.

321
00:11:47,488 --> 00:11:48,892
And there was fitness.

322
00:11:49,072 --> 00:11:51,678
The jogging craze of the late seventies

323
00:11:51,678 --> 00:11:52,880
was in full swing.

324
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:56,280
James Fixx's Complete Book of Running was

325
00:11:56,280 --> 00:11:57,379
a best seller.

326
00:11:57,559 --> 00:12:01,004
Thirty-year-olds bought Nike running shoes and headbands,

327
00:12:01,004 --> 00:12:04,026
and ran through their neighborhoods every morning.

328
00:12:04,206 --> 00:12:08,130
Racquetball clubs and health spas opened everywhere.

329
00:12:08,481 --> 00:12:10,357
Weekends meant cookouts,

330
00:12:10,357 --> 00:12:13,639
softball leagues, bowling and trips to the

331
00:12:13,639 --> 00:12:14,030
lake.

332
00:12:14,210 --> 00:12:17,331
Many thirty-year-olds had grown up camping with

333
00:12:17,331 --> 00:12:19,788
their parents, and now took their own

334
00:12:19,788 --> 00:12:20,120
kids.

335
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:22,514
Others went to rock concerts,

336
00:12:22,514 --> 00:12:24,958
the same bands they had loved at

337
00:12:24,958 --> 00:12:27,707
eighteen, now playing in big arenas.

338
00:12:27,887 --> 00:12:29,942
Fleetwood Mac, the Eagles,

339
00:12:29,942 --> 00:12:33,657
Led Zeppelin, Billy Joel and Bruce Springsteen.

340
00:12:34,007 --> 00:12:37,520
Turning thirty in nineteen seventy-nine meant looking

341
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:39,707
back on a remarkable life so far.

342
00:12:39,887 --> 00:12:42,112
You had seen more change in thirty

343
00:12:42,112 --> 00:12:44,729
years than your grandparents had seen in

344
00:12:44,729 --> 00:12:45,448
a lifetime.

345
00:12:45,628 --> 00:12:47,683
Television, rock and roll,

346
00:12:47,683 --> 00:12:49,738
the civil rights movement,

347
00:12:49,738 --> 00:12:51,792
Vietnam, the moon landing,

348
00:12:51,792 --> 00:12:52,583
Watergate.

349
00:12:52,763 --> 00:12:54,690
And you were only halfway through.

350
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:56,781
It also meant looking ahead.

351
00:12:56,961 --> 00:12:58,319
The eighties were coming.

352
00:12:58,499 --> 00:12:59,858
Within a few years,

353
00:12:59,858 --> 00:13:01,359
inflation would fall,

354
00:13:01,359 --> 00:13:03,002
the economy would boom,

355
00:13:03,002 --> 00:13:06,147
and many of those thirty-year-olds would buy

356
00:13:06,147 --> 00:13:09,006
homes, build careers and raise families.

357
00:13:09,186 --> 00:13:11,186
They would become the parents of a

358
00:13:11,186 --> 00:13:13,008
new generation, and eventually,

359
00:13:13,008 --> 00:13:14,655
the grandparents of another.

360
00:13:15,005 --> 00:13:18,089
If you turned thirty in nineteen seventy-nine,

361
00:13:18,089 --> 00:13:20,368
you're in your late seventies now.

362
00:13:20,548 --> 00:13:22,000
Think back to that year.

363
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:23,352
The gas line.

364
00:13:23,532 --> 00:13:24,391
The disco.

365
00:13:24,571 --> 00:13:26,034
The first VCR.

366
00:13:26,214 --> 00:13:27,595
The little kids at home,

367
00:13:27,595 --> 00:13:29,840
the mortgage you weren't sure you could

368
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:30,243
afford.

369
00:13:30,423 --> 00:13:31,549
You made it through.

370
00:13:31,729 --> 00:13:33,415
And you probably did a lot better

371
00:13:33,415 --> 00:13:34,794
than you thought you would.

372
00:13:35,144 --> 00:13:36,966
So tell us in the comments.

373
00:13:37,146 --> 00:13:39,937
How old were you in nineteen seventy-nine,

374
00:13:39,937 --> 00:13:41,930
and what do you remember most?

375
00:13:42,110 --> 00:13:43,898
Did you wait in a gas line?

376
00:13:44,078 --> 00:13:45,506
Did you go to a disco?

377
00:13:45,686 --> 00:13:47,090
We read every comment.

378
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:49,853
Next time, we're asking what happened to

379
00:13:49,853 --> 00:13:51,531
the door-to-door salesman.

380
00:13:51,711 --> 00:13:53,483
Thank you for spending this time with

381
00:13:53,483 --> 00:13:53,627
me.
